# Database and typed SQL

Odroe provides a small typed relational query layer over explicit database
drivers. It is intentionally not a full identity-map ORM and it does not hide
migrations, connection scope, or provider differences.

## One shared transport

```dart
abstract interface class SqlExecutor {
  Future<List<T>> query<T>(
    BoundSql statement,
    T Function(SqlRow row) decode,
  );

  Future<SqlWriteResult> execute(BoundSql statement);
}

abstract interface class SqlDatabase implements SqlExecutor {
  Future<List<SqlWriteResult>> atomicWrite(List<BoundSql> statements);
  Future<void> close();
}
```

`TransactionalSqlDatabase` is a separate capability. D1 can support atomic
batches without pretending to support an interactive transaction callback.

## Types stop mistakes before I/O

A `SqlTable<T>` owns columns and a projection. `SqlQueries` creates select,
insert, update, and delete statements for an explicit dialect:

```dart
final posts = Posts();
const queries = SqlQueries(SqlDialect.sqlite);

final title = await queries
    .selectTable(posts, where: posts.id.equals(42))
    .oneOrNull(database);
```

Use `all` for collections. `oneOrNull` returns `null` for no row and rejects
multiple rows, preserving an exact zero-or-one cardinality contract.

Column assignment and predicate types are checked by Dart. Joined reads qualify
columns; left joins require an explicitly optional result selection. Updating
or deleting every row requires the `allRows` confirmation token. Mutations stay
single-table.

Generated statements retain their dialect. SQLite and D1 use
`SqlDialect.sqlite`, PostgreSQL uses `postgres`, and MySQL/MariaDB uses `mysql`.
An explicit mismatch throws `SqlException(SqlErrorCode.unsupported)` before
the driver sends the statement.

`BoundSql.parts` is the escape hatch for source-owned SQL. Fragments are
trusted source and values are bound. Pin `dialect` and `kind` when known; Odroe
does not parse arbitrary SQL to invent portability.

## Ownership follows the host

| Runtime | Resource scope | Odroe module contract |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flutter + SQLite | Application | `DatabaseModule.owned`, closed with `AppContext` |
| Native server + SQLite/PostgreSQL/MySQL | Process | requests borrow; `Server.onClose` closes |
| Cloudflare D1 | Invocation | request-owned wrapper around the host binding |

A request-scoped module must not close a shared process pool. A Fetch isolate
has no reliable process-shutdown hook, so D1 remains invocation-scoped.

## Provider status is explicit

| Entrypoint | Status | Current boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `database_sqlite.dart` | Verified | Native CRUD, returning rows, batches, transactions |
| `database_postgres.dart` | Verified | Single connection, bounded pool, transactions |
| `database_mysql.dart` | Preview | MySQL and MariaDB, one serialized connection |
| `database_d1.dart` | Preview | Local Workerd/D1 contract, atomic batches |

All drivers are in the same Dart package but use separate entrypoints so the
wrong implementation is not reachable from a target. Package dependency
resolution and native build hooks still have cost; inspect real artifacts when
binary size matters.

Schema files remain application-owned. D1 uses reviewable Wrangler migrations;
server databases use an application-owned provider migration path. The
generated SQLite bootstrap creates only its fixed initial schema. Odroe does
not add code generation or a migration DSL.

Continue with [Database providers](/docs/guides/database-providers) for working
code, pool ownership, D1 bindings, and the complete runtime matrix.
